<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Buying vs Renting</span>
<h1>Long-stay Tourists: Ownership Cost Planning for Hanoi Motorbikes</h1>
<p>Owning a motorbike for the length of a long visit is a ledger with a known end date, which makes it the rare budget a guide can actually teach rather than merely guess at. The purchase price is the first line, not the last, and behind it wait the repairs, the fluids, the official fees, the risks and the departure, all of them billing inside a fixed window of weeks or months. This guide plans that window honestly: categories instead of invented numbers, official answers left with official sources, and the verified rental figures held up at the end as the only honest ruler the ownership ledger has.</p>
<p>The machines, the briefing and the honest numbers come from Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily 09:00 to 21:00, where the desk has audited visitor budgets for years and prices the debrief in patience rather than dong. Begin at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-for-sale/">motorbike for sale hub</a>, study the long-stay arrangements on the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental</a> page, check the verified figures on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, review the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> for maintenance and safety, and keep <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/">renting a motorbike in Vietnam</a> open for the legal layer.</p>
